WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.586
NARRATOR: A circular venture does more than
add recycling to an otherwise linear business.

2
00:00:05.586 --> 00:00:10.864
NARRATOR: It changes where value is created,
who participates after a sale, and how

3
00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:12.372
products or materials return.

4
00:00:12.372 --> 00:00:17.794
NARRATOR: Begin with a familiar linear chain:
extract a resource, manufacture a product,

5
00:00:17.794 --> 00:00:20.714
sell it, use it, and discard it.

6
00:00:20.714 --> 00:00:24.744
NARRATOR: Every broken connection can destroy
economic and material value.

7
00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:30.503
NARRATOR: A circular entrepreneur asks which
value can be retained: the whole product

8
00:00:30.503 --> 00:00:35.377
through reuse, its function through repair,
its components through refurbishment or

9
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:38.035
remanufacturing, or its material through
recycling.

10
00:00:38.035 --> 00:00:43.142
NARRATOR: The venture opportunity is the
coordinated system that makes one of those

11
00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:43.928
loops reliable.

12
00:00:43.928 --> 00:00:48.864
NARRATOR: This lesson builds that system from
life-cycle mapping through business-model

13
00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:51.556
design, measurement, and a disciplined pilot.

14
00:00:51.674 --> 00:00:55.093
NARRATOR: Opportunity discovery begins with a
boundary.

15
00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:59.453
NARRATOR: NIST's reference model separates
five connected activities: design the product,

16
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:05.794
acquire materials, produce the product, use
and consume it, and treat it at end of life.

17
00:01:05.794 --> 00:01:11.825
NARRATOR: For each stage, record material
inputs, information, responsible actors,

18
00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:14.238
controls, costs, and outputs.

19
00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:19.906
NARRATOR: Then mark where products become
idle, damaged, obsolete, contaminated,

20
00:01:19.906 --> 00:01:23.307
geographically dispersed, or difficult to
identify.

21
00:01:23.307 --> 00:01:29.033
NARRATOR: A discarded item at one stage may be
a valuable feedstock or service opportunity at

22
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:29.391
another.

23
00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:31.930
NARRATOR: Do not draw only the forward flow.

24
00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:36.896
NARRATOR: Downstream information—failure
modes, repair history, composition, ownership,

25
00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:41.241
and residual performance—can improve the next
design.

26
00:01:41.241 --> 00:01:46.264
NARRATOR: A useful map makes missing
relationships visible before a team commits to

27
00:01:46.264 --> 00:01:47.810
a circular business model.

28
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.890
NARRATOR: Circular strategies are not
interchangeable.

29
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.002
NARRATOR: Reuse keeps the product and its
function intact.

30
00:01:54.002 --> 00:01:56.438
NARRATOR: Repair restores a failed function.

31
00:01:56.438 --> 00:01:59.869
NARRATOR: Refurbishment updates or replaces
selected parts.

32
00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:04.755
NARRATOR: Remanufacturing rebuilds a product
or component to a defined condition.

33
00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:09.999
NARRATOR: Recycling recovers material after
more of the original form and embedded effort

34
00:02:09.999 --> 00:02:11.209
have been lost.

35
00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:16.442
NARRATOR: The shortest loop is not
automatically feasible, but each extra

36
00:02:16.442 --> 00:02:20.724
processing step can add cost, energy,
uncertainty, and coordination.

37
00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:26.901
NARRATOR: Evaluate condition, remaining useful
life, safety, customer expectations, technical

38
00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:29.372
access, and recovery logistics.

39
00:02:29.372 --> 00:02:34.413
NARRATOR: A venture should state exactly which
object returns, in what condition, through

40
00:02:34.413 --> 00:02:37.127
which channel, and to what next use.

41
00:02:37.127 --> 00:02:40.341
NARRATOR: Saying "we recycle" is not a
business design.

42
00:02:40.341 --> 00:02:44.270
NARRATOR: Choosing a loop means choosing an
operational promise that can be tested.

43
00:02:44.402 --> 00:02:49.403
NARRATOR: An entrepreneur needs evidence that
a loop contains enough recoverable value.

44
00:02:49.403 --> 00:02:54.747
NARRATOR: A material-flow view asks how many
units enter the market, where they travel,

45
00:02:54.747 --> 00:02:59.709
when they leave use, what condition they
retain, and what fraction can realistically

46
00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:00.091
return.

47
00:03:00.091 --> 00:03:04.946
NARRATOR: A life-cycle view asks where
environmental burdens move when the proposed

48
00:03:04.946 --> 00:03:05.755
loop changes.

49
00:03:05.755 --> 00:03:11.233
NARRATOR: Pair those views with business
questions: who pays today, who bears disposal

50
00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:16.290
cost, who benefits from longer life, and who
controls the customer relationship?

51
00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.388
NARRATOR: Avoid using one attractive recovery
percentage without a denominator or time

52
00:03:21.388 --> 00:03:21.813
period.

53
00:03:21.813 --> 00:03:27.048
NARRATOR: A small stream of high-value
components may support a venture better than a

54
00:03:27.048 --> 00:03:28.918
large stream of contaminated material.

55
00:03:28.918 --> 00:03:34.113
NARRATOR: The purpose of the map is to locate
a testable mismatch between wasted value and

56
00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.711
an actor willing to pay for its retention.

57
00:03:36.842 --> 00:03:41.792
NARRATOR: A viable circular venture must
create value for more than one stakeholder.

58
00:03:41.792 --> 00:03:47.458
NARRATOR: The customer may receive lower total
cost, reliable maintenance, convenient

59
00:03:47.458 --> 00:03:51.064
take-back, verified quality, or access without
ownership.

60
00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:57.493
NARRATOR: The venture may earn through resale
margin, repair fees, subscriptions, leasing,

61
00:03:57.493 --> 00:04:00.707
deposits, service contracts, or recovered
components.

62
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:05.567
NARRATOR: Suppliers may gain stable secondary
materials or better failure data.

63
00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:10.298
NARRATOR: Environmental value may come from
avoided extraction, longer product life,

64
00:04:10.298 --> 00:04:16.320
reduced waste, or more efficient recovery, but
it must be measured rather than assumed.

65
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.188
NARRATOR: Write the value proposition as a
causal statement: because the venture performs

66
00:04:22.188 --> 00:04:27.604
a specific recovery activity for a defined
customer, a measurable cost, risk,

67
00:04:27.604 --> 00:04:30.312
convenience, performance, or resource outcome
changes.

68
00:04:30.312 --> 00:04:33.705
NARRATOR: That statement becomes a hypothesis,
not a slogan.

69
00:04:33.866 --> 00:04:39.632
NARRATOR: Circularity becomes expensive when a
product was designed only for first sale.

70
00:04:39.632 --> 00:04:44.741
NARRATOR: Fasteners may be inaccessible,
components inseparable, materials

71
00:04:44.741 --> 00:04:49.211
unidentified, software locked, or failure data
unavailable.

72
00:04:49.211 --> 00:04:54.430
NARRATOR: NIST's model emphasizes using
downstream life-cycle information to improve

73
00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:55.474
product design.

74
00:04:55.474 --> 00:05:02.651
NARRATOR: For a venture, that means deciding
which data must travel with a product:

75
00:05:02.651 --> 00:05:06.752
composition, serial identity, maintenance
history, disassembly instructions, ownership,

76
00:05:06.752 --> 00:05:08.803
condition, and safety status.

77
00:05:08.803 --> 00:05:14.032
NARRATOR: It also means identifying who may
read or update that information and who pays

78
00:05:14.032 --> 00:05:15.078
to maintain it.

79
00:05:15.078 --> 00:05:20.855
NARRATOR: A modular design can make repair
easier, but only if replacement parts,

80
00:05:20.855 --> 00:05:23.522
diagnostics, labor, and reverse logistics
exist.

81
00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:27.770
NARRATOR: Product design and business design
therefore form one system.

82
00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.719
NARRATOR: Each proposed feature should be tied
to a recovery decision it enables.

83
00:05:32.858 --> 00:05:36.404
NARRATOR: A circular model fails when the
return path is vague.

84
00:05:36.404 --> 00:05:43.419
NARRATOR: Specify collection points, shipping
or pickup, inspection, grading, data capture,

85
00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:47.246
storage, repair, redistribution, and final
recovery.

86
00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.236
NARRATOR: Then align incentives.

87
00:05:49.236 --> 00:05:51.813
A customer needs a reason to return the item.

88
00:05:51.813 --> 00:05:55.142
NARRATOR: A service partner needs compensation
and standards.

89
00:05:55.142 --> 00:05:59.899
NARRATOR: A buyer of recovered output needs
predictable quantity and quality.

90
00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:05.639
NARRATOR: The venture needs enough margin to
absorb failed returns, transport, testing,

91
00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:07.552
cleaning, and warranty risk.

92
00:06:07.552 --> 00:06:13.853
NARRATOR: Safety and regulatory requirements
can change which items may be reused or

93
00:06:13.853 --> 00:06:14.338
remanufactured.

94
00:06:14.338 --> 00:06:20.813
NARRATOR: Build the reverse network around
observable states: received, inspected,

95
00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:25.346
accepted, routed, restored, resold, or
responsibly rejected.

96
00:06:25.346 --> 00:06:27.846
NARRATOR: Coordination is part of the product.

97
00:06:27.846 --> 00:06:33.072
NARRATOR: Without it, even a technically
recoverable item can remain economically

98
00:06:33.072 --> 00:06:33.547
stranded.

99
00:06:33.698 --> 00:06:37.270
NARRATOR: One circular-looking metric can hide
a worse system result.

100
00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.905
NARRATOR: A high collection rate does not
prove that products are reused.

101
00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:44.273
NARRATOR: Recycled content does not reveal
durability.

102
00:06:44.273 --> 00:06:47.920
NARRATOR: Longer life may require added
materials or energy.

103
00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.795
NARRATOR: A repair service may reduce waste
but add transport, delays, and rejected

104
00:06:53.795 --> 00:06:54.247
inventory.

105
00:06:54.247 --> 00:07:00.647
NARRATOR: Measure the chain with a small
balanced set: return rate, usable recovery

106
00:07:00.647 --> 00:07:06.061
yield, additional life delivered, virgin
material displaced, process losses, cost per

107
00:07:06.061 --> 00:07:10.984
recovered unit, customer retention, safety
failures, and environmental indicators within

108
00:07:10.984 --> 00:07:12.461
a declared boundary.

109
00:07:12.461 --> 00:07:17.968
NARRATOR: Compare against a credible baseline,
not against doing nothing in an imaginary

110
00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:18.392
system.

111
00:07:18.392 --> 00:07:21.288
NARRATOR: State uncertainty and data gaps.

112
00:07:21.288 --> 00:07:24.247
NARRATOR: The question is not whether one loop
exists.

113
00:07:24.247 --> 00:07:29.497
NARRATOR: It is whether the venture produces
better environmental, customer, and business

114
00:07:29.497 --> 00:07:32.997
outcomes without shifting burdens outside the
measurement boundary.

115
00:07:33.122 --> 00:07:37.969
NARRATOR: Consider a hypothetical venture that
leases modular laboratory equipment.

116
00:07:37.969 --> 00:07:43.114
NARRATOR: Its promise is reliable access and
rapid maintenance rather than permanent

117
00:07:43.114 --> 00:07:43.543
ownership.

118
00:07:43.543 --> 00:07:48.046
NARRATOR: Returned units are identified by
serial number, inspected, and routed.

119
00:07:48.046 --> 00:07:51.018
NARRATOR: A working unit is cleaned and
redeployed.

120
00:07:51.018 --> 00:07:55.010
NARRATOR: A repairable unit receives a
documented component replacement.

121
00:07:55.010 --> 00:08:00.816
NARRATOR: An unsafe unit supplies verified
components before remaining materials enter an

122
00:08:00.816 --> 00:08:02.268
appropriate recovery stream.

123
00:08:02.268 --> 00:08:06.515
NARRATOR: The example is not proof that
leasing is always circular.

124
00:08:06.515 --> 00:08:12.883
NARRATOR: The team must test return behavior,
utilization, transport distance, repair time,

125
00:08:12.883 --> 00:08:18.720
component availability, residual value, energy
use, and customer willingness to accept

126
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:19.781
refurbished equipment.

127
00:08:19.781 --> 00:08:25.087
NARRATOR: A pilot might follow twenty units
through one semester and compare service,

128
00:08:25.087 --> 00:08:28.760
cost, and material outcomes with the current
purchase-and-discard process.

129
00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.727
NARRATOR: The model earns credibility through
traceable operations.

130
00:08:32.858 --> 00:08:36.774
NARRATOR: Before calling a venture circular,
answer ten questions.

131
00:08:36.774 --> 00:08:40.282
NARRATOR: What product and life-cycle boundary
are in scope?

132
00:08:40.282 --> 00:08:41.953
NARRATOR: Which actor owns the problem?

133
00:08:41.953 --> 00:08:44.797
NARRATOR: What exactly returns, and in what
condition?

134
00:08:44.797 --> 00:08:48.547
NARRATOR: Which value-retention strategy is
technically feasible?

135
00:08:48.547 --> 00:08:52.399
NARRATOR: Who receives environmental,
consumer, and business value?

136
00:08:52.399 --> 00:08:57.336
NARRATOR: How will collection, inspection,
processing, and redistribution work?

137
00:08:57.336 --> 00:09:00.244
NARRATOR: What information must move with the
product?

138
00:09:00.244 --> 00:09:03.241
NARRATOR: Which baseline and metrics will test
the claim?

139
00:09:03.241 --> 00:09:08.917
NARRATOR: What safety, incentive, market, and
burden-shifting risks could invalidate it?

140
00:09:08.917 --> 00:09:14.619
NARRATOR: Finally, what is the smallest pilot
that can produce decision-quality evidence?

141
00:09:14.619 --> 00:09:20.333
NARRATOR: End with a decision rule: expand,
revise, or stop based on measured outcomes.

142
00:09:20.333 --> 00:09:24.593
NARRATOR: Circular entrepreneurship is not the
promise of a perfect loop.

143
00:09:24.593 --> 00:09:30.365
NARRATOR: It is the disciplined design and
testing of a system that retains more value

144
00:09:30.365 --> 00:09:31.519
with credible benefits.
